Author: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date:   Tue Aug 25 21:01:55 2026 +0000

    Fail closed when an upstream provider misbehaves
    
    Four adapter-layer defects, all in the same family: a provider that
    degrades quietly produced a plausible-looking result instead of an error.
    
    1. No timeout on ESPN public odds, TheRundown, or MLB Stats API. Every
       other upstream client in packages/data-ingestion caps its calls at
       12-15s; these three did not. A hung socket held the ingest cron open
       until the platform killed the function, and a killed cron writes
       nothing, logs nothing and reports nothing. ESPN issues up to 29
       serial calls per sport, so one hang stalls the whole fetch.
    
    2. MLB Stats API returned [] on any HTTP error, timeout or parse
       failure, making an outage identical to a league with no standings.
       The only consumer (tryMlbStandingsFairValue) caches whatever it gets
       for 30 minutes BEFORE inspecting it, so a single 500 poisoned that
       cache with an empty table and silently suppressed MLB independent
       fair values for half an hour. Now throws MlbStatsApiError; [] is
       reserved for a genuinely empty publication.
    
    3. TheRundown skips a day of its span on 4xx/5xx and abandons the rest
       on 429, then returned the survivors with only a free-text
       "partial: ..." note. process-sport uses that result to REPLACE the
       whole slate when the primary is empty, so a truncated span was
       written as the complete board: the run reported SUCCESS, the
       freshness clock advanced, and the dropped games looked unscheduled.
       The result now carries complete/failedDays, and the board-replacement
       branch refuses an incomplete fetch. The thin-fill branch is
       deliberately unchanged - it only adds books to games already held.
    
    4. The Odds API decoded every 2xx with a bare `as T` cast. A 200
       carrying an object left events.length === undefined, and because
       `undefined === 0` is false the "primary empty, use the free paths"
       branch never fired: both fallbacks were skipped and the run died with
       an opaque TypeError in the normalizer. A 200 carrying HTML threw a
       raw SyntaxError that escaped every OddsApiError classification.
       Array endpoints now assert an array and the JSON parse is wrapped.
    
    Every fix ships with a test watched failing against pre-fix code:
    transports stubbing a 500 with an HTML body, a 429 with Retry-After, a
    200 with {}, a 200 with a foreign shape, and a hang. Stubs are local and
    throw on any unexpected host - no live provider is contacted.
    
    Verified on Node 20.20.2 (CI pin): typecheck clean across types,
    data-ingestion, ingestion-pipeline, prediction-engine and apps/web;
    data-ingestion 361 tests pass; ingestion-pipeline 223 pass; guardrails
    green.
    
